1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:07,880
Two and a half miles long, a thousand feet high, and 120,000 tons of high-strength steel.

2
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:20,280
Challenged by typhoon and earthquake, it's the longest, heaviest, and most expensive suspension bridge ever built.

3
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:24,460
Now, the world's longest bridge on Modern Marvels.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,980
It's one of the most violent waterways in the world.

5
00:00:40,120 --> 00:00:44,120
Wrecked by typhoons, cursed with driving seas,

6
00:00:44,620 --> 00:00:47,220
and with currents reaching 15 feet per second,

7
00:00:47,740 --> 00:00:50,700
it's the Akashi Strait in Japan's inland sea.

8
00:00:51,620 --> 00:00:54,560
Long a major barrier to uniting northern and southern Japan,

9
00:00:55,480 --> 00:01:00,560
This two-and-a-half-mile watery divide is now spanned by the mightiest bridge in the world.

10
00:01:01,460 --> 00:01:07,500
The most technologically advanced suspension design ever built, the Akashi-Kaikyo Bridge.

11
00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:14,660
The Japanese were presented with a very interesting challenge in the Akashi Straits.

12
00:01:14,900 --> 00:01:16,940
It's a relatively narrow strait.

13
00:01:18,440 --> 00:01:21,220
It's a major shipping channel.

14
00:01:21,221 --> 00:01:27,600
1,400 commercial vessels that pass through the straits every single day.

15
00:01:28,480 --> 00:01:33,920
Coupled with that, then, is the currents or the flow of water through the Akashi Straits.

16
00:01:34,480 --> 00:01:37,940
The Akashi Channel was a major challenge to Japan's bridge builders.

17
00:01:38,440 --> 00:01:41,400
The strait is over 360 feet deep.

18
00:01:41,820 --> 00:01:45,820
The intense currents that course through it cause violent flows at the surface

19
00:01:45,821 --> 00:01:48,360
and rapid erosion on the seabed.

20
00:01:48,940 --> 00:01:52,880
It is crowded with both fast-moving freight and stationary fishing vessels.

21
00:01:53,820 --> 00:01:59,940
And in the center of this, the engineers propose to situate two nearly 1,000-foot-high bridge towers

22
00:01:59,941 --> 00:02:05,720
and join them with a record-breaking 12,828-foot span, which would link north to south.

23
00:02:06,080 --> 00:02:08,780
Economic development is very important to the Japanese,

24
00:02:08,781 --> 00:02:14,800
and of course the populated areas of Tokyo and Osaka have the population density.

25
00:02:14,801 --> 00:02:21,800
The Japanese want to open up for economic development many of the other parts of their country.

26
00:02:21,801 --> 00:02:29,800
And so to do that, they have to get from the population centers out into what was once the hinterlands.

27
00:02:29,801 --> 00:02:38,800
And so they established a transportation network comprised largely of rail and highway.

28
00:02:38,801 --> 00:02:46,020
The Japanese take that experience that they developed on the other bridges that they built in the Honshu-Shikoku bridge projects,

29
00:02:46,180 --> 00:02:49,880
leading to what is now the longest suspension bridge in the world.

30
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:55,680
These mini-bridge projects have all been designed to face a final immense challenge.

31
00:02:56,640 --> 00:03:00,500
Earthquakes, and of all the natural enemies posed by the environment of Japan,

32
00:03:01,540 --> 00:03:06,440
none is so feared or deadly as the quakes that shake the island nation frequently.

33
00:03:06,441 --> 00:03:10,760
Most of them are small, passing almost unnoticed.

34
00:03:11,720 --> 00:03:14,860
But on the early morning of January 17, 1995,

35
00:03:15,480 --> 00:03:19,340
the Great Kobe Earthquake crashed through the Akashi Strait

36
00:03:19,341 --> 00:03:21,820
into the slumbering city.

37
00:03:24,380 --> 00:03:26,440
Then the Kobe Earthquake occurred.

38
00:03:27,480 --> 00:03:30,340
When we surveyed the center line of the bridge,

39
00:03:30,580 --> 00:03:34,900
we discovered that it was slightly off the straight line.

40
00:03:34,901 --> 00:03:40,520
Today, this magnificent bridge connects the main island of Japan with its southern cousins.

41
00:03:41,460 --> 00:03:44,980
Traffic crosses its six lanes day and night in all kinds of weather.

42
00:03:45,880 --> 00:03:50,800
And below, hundreds of ships carrying all kinds of commercial cargo pass unimpeded.

43
00:03:51,180 --> 00:03:52,380
But it was not always so.

44
00:03:58,220 --> 00:04:02,920
Early in its history, Japan was a nation divided by and bounded by water.

45
00:04:03,720 --> 00:04:08,200
In the mid-1800s, attempts were made to bring Japan into parody with the West.

46
00:04:09,780 --> 00:04:14,100
With Britain entering the Victorian age and the Industrial Revolution sweeping America,

47
00:04:14,740 --> 00:04:17,980
Japan looked to the West for technical inspiration and expertise.

48
00:04:19,120 --> 00:04:23,920
By the 20th century, modern steamships had begun to link the many islands of the Japanese archipelago.

49
00:04:25,540 --> 00:04:30,280
But ferries and smaller vessels often fell victim to the whims of weather and sea conditions.

50
00:04:31,240 --> 00:04:35,860
By the late 1800s, Japanese engineers were traveling to the United States in Great Britain

51
00:04:35,861 --> 00:04:39,500
to observe the building of some of the great suspension bridges of that time,

52
00:04:40,520 --> 00:04:42,520
including the Brooklyn Bridge in New York.

53
00:04:43,640 --> 00:04:45,860
They took this expertise home to Japan,

54
00:04:46,440 --> 00:04:50,960
but it would be nearly 60 years before it would be put into practice on very large projects.

55
00:04:51,780 --> 00:04:57,040
By the late 1930s, plans for large-scale bridge construction were on the drawing boards.

56
00:04:57,041 --> 00:05:02,100
but World War II would divert resources

57
00:05:02,101 --> 00:05:08,000
manpower and willpower from the task

58
00:05:08,001 --> 00:05:16,440
by the time it was over

59
00:05:16,441 --> 00:05:19,480
two million of Japan's sons and daughters were gone

60
00:05:19,481 --> 00:05:21,420
and the nation lay in ruins

61
00:05:21,421 --> 00:05:34,600
all of their efforts really began post-world war ii the japanese began their quest for building

62
00:05:34,601 --> 00:05:41,560
large bridges and structures spanning the straits spanning the waters the seas of japan

63
00:05:41,561 --> 00:05:46,640
in about the early 1950s.

64
00:05:47,360 --> 00:05:51,460
And what the Japanese do is they take a large project

65
00:05:51,461 --> 00:05:56,480
and they research it, they practice, they build that bridge,

66
00:05:56,540 --> 00:05:58,440
and from that bridge, they learn a lot more.

67
00:05:58,540 --> 00:06:01,220
They take that experience that they developed on that bridge

68
00:06:01,221 --> 00:06:04,180
and they turn it into the other bridges that they built

69
00:06:04,181 --> 00:06:06,520
in the Honshu Shikoku bridge projects.

70
00:06:06,740 --> 00:06:10,140
In the 1950s, the future of Japan's economy depended

71
00:06:10,141 --> 00:06:12,320
on linking the country's sprawling infrastructure.

72
00:06:13,020 --> 00:06:14,620
We need infrastructures.

73
00:06:15,180 --> 00:06:18,700
We have so many islands separated by sea

74
00:06:18,701 --> 00:06:25,140
so that in order to connect each side, we need bridges.

75
00:06:26,180 --> 00:06:29,400
But the seaways of Japan would not be easily bridged.

76
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:33,520
Many of these waters, the prime lifeline of commerce for the island nation,

77
00:06:33,521 --> 00:06:36,440
are infamous for their strong currents and foul weather.

78
00:06:37,300 --> 00:06:38,940
This made boat traffic unreliable.

79
00:06:39,600 --> 00:06:42,280
But building a bridge to replace it seemed a daunting task.

80
00:06:43,100 --> 00:06:46,680
While other narrower routes between the islands had been spanned by bridges,

81
00:06:47,080 --> 00:06:51,180
by the late 1980s, the Akashi Strait was still served by ferryboat.

82
00:06:51,760 --> 00:06:56,160
The government wanted to open this one last key route to the south, if they could.

83
00:06:56,740 --> 00:07:04,200
The Akashi Bridge is just a huge project that had never been attempted from the magnitude of scale.

84
00:07:04,201 --> 00:07:10,200
So a lot of the technologies that worked before, a lot of the standard construction technologies,

85
00:07:10,700 --> 00:07:16,200
a lot of the design criteria had to be modified, had to be redeveloped,

86
00:07:17,120 --> 00:07:21,360
and had to be tailored specifically to this creation, the Akashi Bridge.

87
00:07:24,300 --> 00:07:28,000
Bridging the treacherous Akashi Strait was easier said than done.

88
00:07:28,500 --> 00:07:30,640
Home to both severe wind and waves,

89
00:07:30,641 --> 00:07:34,920
A bridge here would have to be designed to withstand the worst of these combined forces.

90
00:07:35,840 --> 00:07:40,420
The Akashi-Kaikyo Bridge would be the last and longest span in this violent environment.

91
00:07:41,100 --> 00:07:42,760
But what kind of bridge should be built?

92
00:07:43,680 --> 00:07:47,740
When looking at span lengths so great over one kilometer,

93
00:07:48,240 --> 00:07:55,120
a suspension bridge is the only viable structural type capable of spanning such great distances.

94
00:07:56,080 --> 00:07:58,920
But structural considerations were not the only challenge.

95
00:07:59,380 --> 00:08:02,240
Japan is a country with a keenly developed sense of the aesthetic,

96
00:08:02,660 --> 00:08:04,500
and this applied to the bridge design as well.

97
00:08:05,080 --> 00:08:10,500
The Japanese are very, very conscious about the environmental aspects of construction,

98
00:08:11,080 --> 00:08:16,500
and they are very, very conscientious about the aesthetics of any of their projects that they do.

99
00:08:16,501 --> 00:08:23,060
The government and the population wanted a bridge that blended and fit in to the environment.

100
00:08:23,560 --> 00:08:27,880
I think most anyone would acknowledge that the form of a suspension bridge

101
00:08:27,881 --> 00:08:34,140
which is perhaps one of the most magnificent shapes and forms of any kind of bridge that you could possibly design.

102
00:08:34,840 --> 00:08:38,500
The 2.5-mile span would be crossed with a suspension bridge.

103
00:08:39,600 --> 00:08:43,680
With this determination made, they had one other major design concern.

104
00:08:47,280 --> 00:08:47,980
Earthquakes.

105
00:08:48,360 --> 00:08:51,260
Japan as a whole is seismically extremely active.

106
00:08:51,261 --> 00:08:57,200
It's on the Pacific Rim, so the Pacific Blade is rotating by about two inches every year.

107
00:08:57,201 --> 00:09:00,580
And so this causes then earthquakes along the entire Pacific Rim.

108
00:09:00,700 --> 00:09:03,740
And so Japan is prone to see a lot of earthquakes.

109
00:09:04,220 --> 00:09:09,220
Also, the site where the Akashikaiko Bridge is clearly is exposed to seismic threats.

110
00:09:09,580 --> 00:09:15,440
So it was recognized that there was a fault running in through the straits there.

111
00:09:15,540 --> 00:09:18,580
It runs along the periphery of the city of Kobe.

112
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:25,260
It wasn't thought to be a very important fault at the time of the first construction of the bridge.

113
00:09:25,880 --> 00:09:29,720
To ensure a safe design, bridge planners carried out extensive testing.

114
00:09:30,400 --> 00:09:36,100
As the Japanese investigated seismic design,

115
00:09:36,460 --> 00:09:39,180
they relied very heavily on computational models.

116
00:09:39,181 --> 00:09:43,980
The advent of the computer was just one of the marvels of recent time.

117
00:09:44,420 --> 00:09:47,240
Computational power is, without a doubt,

118
00:09:47,420 --> 00:09:51,660
saving grace for being able to design these huge structures.

119
00:09:51,661 --> 00:09:58,580
And so computationally is the best way to investigate the seismic performance and seismic behavior.

120
00:09:59,260 --> 00:10:00,460
The data looked promising.

121
00:10:01,160 --> 00:10:03,960
The computers had vetted the most critical design considerations.

122
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:08,040
A bridge across the Akashi Strait looked possible.

123
00:10:08,500 --> 00:10:10,480
But now, they had to build it.

124
00:10:12,860 --> 00:10:17,800
Among its other records, the Akashi-Kaikyo Bridge is also one of the most expensive to cross,

125
00:10:18,260 --> 00:10:19,920
at over $20 per vehicle.

126
00:10:19,921 --> 00:10:23,920
The world's longest bridge will return on Modern Marvels.

127
00:10:28,300 --> 00:10:30,720
Suspension bridges are in their simplest form.

128
00:10:31,280 --> 00:10:33,960
Huge horizontal decks hanging from overhead cables.

129
00:10:34,940 --> 00:10:37,160
These cables must be supported by the shoreline.

130
00:10:38,160 --> 00:10:42,160
To do this, giant anchoring points, or anchorages, must be fabricated.

131
00:10:42,540 --> 00:10:45,340
This was the first step to creating the Akashi Kaikyo Bridge.

132
00:10:45,800 --> 00:10:52,720
When you design a suspension bridge, whatever the total weight of the bridge is,

133
00:10:53,200 --> 00:10:55,460
it has to be carried in those anchorages.

134
00:10:55,920 --> 00:10:58,000
If you've got 1,000 pounds you're carrying,

135
00:10:59,020 --> 00:11:03,900
it's not inconceivable that the size of the anchorage, the mass of the anchorage,

136
00:11:03,980 --> 00:11:07,560
because that's what resists the total load, would be ten times the amount.

137
00:11:08,160 --> 00:11:11,880
One of these gigantic structures would be built on the Kobe side of the strait,

138
00:11:12,220 --> 00:11:14,240
the other on the Awadji Island side.

139
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:17,440
Each location posed specific challenges.

140
00:11:18,300 --> 00:11:22,640
The first major problem was to get down to hard rock for a firm foundation on which to build.

141
00:11:25,320 --> 00:11:28,060
But bedrock was a scarce commodity in the area.

142
00:11:28,600 --> 00:11:35,140
Typically we go far enough down that we do find sufficiently strong material to support the structure.

143
00:11:37,360 --> 00:11:41,940
In fact, only the southern anchorage on Awaji Island would actually touch bedrock.

144
00:11:41,941 --> 00:11:47,500
The rest of the bridge's supporting structures were all built onto gravelly alluvium, or

145
00:11:47,501 --> 00:11:49,500
atop loose strata.

146
00:11:49,501 --> 00:11:57,540
The huge pit for the north anchorage was excavated to a depth of about 200 feet.

147
00:11:57,541 --> 00:12:04,860
Once the dig was complete, the pit wall was prepared by installing a retaining wall.

148
00:12:04,861 --> 00:12:08,480
This eight foot thick wall held back the dirt around the pit.

149
00:12:08,481 --> 00:12:12,760
the pouring of the foundation concrete could begin.

150
00:12:12,761 --> 00:12:18,080
When the excavation process is finished and we reach the bottom, we filled the lower compacted

151
00:12:18,081 --> 00:12:19,820
concrete inside.

152
00:12:19,821 --> 00:12:24,380
In three months, we finished up to within six meters of ground level.

153
00:12:24,381 --> 00:12:27,360
Then we put down steel reinforcement.

154
00:12:27,361 --> 00:12:32,000
300,000 tons of concrete were poured into the pit, making it the biggest structure of

155
00:12:32,001 --> 00:12:34,400
its kind on record.

156
00:12:34,401 --> 00:12:38,240
The concrete was poured in steps to allow each layer to be tamped by steamroller,

157
00:12:38,700 --> 00:12:43,480
and also to avoid a common problem encountered in building large concrete structures, heating.

158
00:12:44,260 --> 00:12:48,580
You need to cool down large masses of concrete when you pour them,

159
00:12:48,900 --> 00:12:52,260
otherwise you get already just from the shrinkage in the concrete.

160
00:12:53,540 --> 00:12:56,900
During the curing process, you get a lot of cracking in the concrete.

161
00:12:57,740 --> 00:12:59,520
Once the foundation was poured,

162
00:12:59,521 --> 00:13:06,120
Steel reinforcement in the top layer would provide connecting points for the huge anchorage tower to be placed on top.

163
00:13:07,120 --> 00:13:11,560
This structure was brought in as one pre-assembled unit by barge-mounted crane.

164
00:13:14,060 --> 00:13:21,320
Since it was close to the ocean, the frames were made in a factory and transported by a floating crane and erected at once.

165
00:13:22,400 --> 00:13:25,360
After that, the anchor frames were set in place.

166
00:13:25,820 --> 00:13:29,180
To do this, we used a high-performance concrete.

167
00:13:29,520 --> 00:13:32,520
The framework in the anchorage structure was so intricate

168
00:13:32,521 --> 00:13:36,520
that traditional concrete would have had trouble flowing between the frame members.

169
00:13:37,520 --> 00:13:40,520
So a new, highly fluid concrete was developed

170
00:13:40,521 --> 00:13:43,520
that would flow easily throughout the structure.

171
00:13:43,521 --> 00:13:46,520
From the top protruded the cable anchorage frame.

172
00:13:46,521 --> 00:13:50,520
This provided the points with which the huge suspension cables would later mate.

173
00:13:50,521 --> 00:13:55,520
If you were to look at the anchorage, you will see that there's the splay block

174
00:13:55,521 --> 00:14:02,660
and then those cables are separated of the main cable and individually anchored inside that anchorage frame.

175
00:14:03,340 --> 00:14:07,000
Once locked to the anchorage, the bridge would transmit most of its weight there.

176
00:14:07,840 --> 00:14:11,740
If anything went wrong here, the bridge would collapse instantly.

177
00:14:12,820 --> 00:14:16,560
When the last batch of concrete was poured and the anchorage proclaimed complete,

178
00:14:17,580 --> 00:14:20,460
the workers held the first of many celebrations.

179
00:14:21,280 --> 00:14:25,540
The south anchorage on Awaji Island was built using the same methodologies,

180
00:14:25,960 --> 00:14:30,760
but the pit was only about 60 feet deep and rested atop granite bedrock.

181
00:14:36,800 --> 00:14:40,440
Next, far from the safety of shore, came the towers.

182
00:14:41,600 --> 00:14:46,420
The towers for the Akashi-Kaikyo Bridge alone required two major contractors each.

183
00:14:46,421 --> 00:14:56,120
The design of the foundations for the two towers, which were to be constructed in the 200-foot deep waters of the channel, would be critical.

184
00:14:56,121 --> 00:15:06,020
Whether it be for the earthquakes, whether it be for the typhoons that come along frequently in Japan,

185
00:15:06,021 --> 00:15:14,020
the high winds, the rain, the treacherous torrents of the waters within the Straits of Akashi,

186
00:15:14,620 --> 00:15:19,300
all of those features had to be accommodated in design and in construction.

187
00:15:19,301 --> 00:15:25,880
The channel between the anchorages was deep and gravel lined with bedrock far below the

188
00:15:25,881 --> 00:15:30,980
point where excavation for tower foundations would be practical.

189
00:15:30,981 --> 00:15:35,180
But the builders wanted to find the most stable surface possible, and these towers would be

190
00:15:35,181 --> 00:15:40,300
the biggest in the world at 930 feet in height.

191
00:15:40,301 --> 00:15:45,160
The solution would involve setting a huge steel cylinder known as a caisson onto the

192
00:15:45,161 --> 00:15:49,160
gravelly bottom to provide a strong stable footprint for each tower.

193
00:15:49,161 --> 00:16:00,160
The caissons are approximately 65 meters or so below sea level and they are approximately

194
00:16:00,161 --> 00:16:03,160
80 meters in diameter.

195
00:16:03,161 --> 00:16:04,160
They're huge.

196
00:16:04,161 --> 00:16:09,160
The Japanese had to accommodate, number one, the tidal currents which were as much as four

197
00:16:09,161 --> 00:16:12,160
and a half meters per second.

198
00:16:12,161 --> 00:16:19,680
What they decided to do was to actually prefabricate huge double-walled steel caissons, two of

199
00:16:19,681 --> 00:16:21,420
them, one for each tower.

200
00:16:21,421 --> 00:16:26,780
And they built those steel caissons on the fabrication plant.

201
00:16:26,781 --> 00:16:32,960
Each 19,000-ton caisson would be sunk and filled with concrete to supply a firm base.

202
00:16:32,961 --> 00:16:38,240
But resting on gravel introduced other problems, such as scouring, where the swift currents

203
00:16:38,241 --> 00:16:41,320
would wash out the gravel beneath the caisson.

204
00:16:41,321 --> 00:16:51,940
Since I knew that the prevention of scouring was the greatest challenge, we conducted laboratory

205
00:16:51,941 --> 00:16:56,100
tests with 1-100 scale models for almost three years.

206
00:16:56,101 --> 00:17:02,000
As a result of the test, we determined that the anti-scouring measure would be to install

207
00:17:02,001 --> 00:17:06,040
80-meter wide rip-wraps around the caisson.

208
00:17:06,041 --> 00:17:10,200
Rip-wrap is a term for mounds of large rocks which would surround the caisson on the sea

209
00:17:10,201 --> 00:17:10,901
floor.

210
00:17:10,902 --> 00:17:17,200
First the sea floor would be dredged using a traditional floating dredging unit.

211
00:17:29,720 --> 00:17:34,542
Then once a circular hole was made, the caisson could be sunk.

212
00:17:34,543 --> 00:17:36,720
The rocks or riprap would be

213
00:17:36,721 --> 00:17:40,360
The rocks or riprap would be dropped around the caisson.

214
00:17:41,160 --> 00:17:42,800
But there was still a problem.

215
00:17:43,880 --> 00:17:48,760
With stones of that size, the sand between them would get sucked up.

216
00:17:48,900 --> 00:17:52,920
To deal with that problem, we came up with filter units.

217
00:17:53,420 --> 00:17:58,080
These filter units were bags of smaller rock that would sit atop the other stones,

218
00:17:58,480 --> 00:18:00,100
forcing the water to flow through them.

219
00:18:00,680 --> 00:18:03,580
This either diverted or slowed the flow of water to the point

220
00:18:03,581 --> 00:18:06,840
that the scouring or removal of seabed was practically eliminated.

221
00:18:07,560 --> 00:18:09,980
The caissons would be able to sit on a gravelly bottom

222
00:18:09,981 --> 00:18:12,380
without losing their footing and twisting out of position.

223
00:18:12,880 --> 00:18:14,620
Tests showed this approach to be sound.

224
00:18:15,360 --> 00:18:19,040
Now the caissons had to be completed and towed into position for sinking.

225
00:18:20,920 --> 00:18:23,860
The caisson, which is 80 meters in diameter,

226
00:18:24,340 --> 00:18:28,580
is divided into two sections, the double wall and the inner core.

227
00:18:28,581 --> 00:18:33,580
The area of the inner core is about 2,500 cubic meters.

228
00:18:35,380 --> 00:18:40,560
We conducted training for the submerging process at Shodo Island for about a month.

229
00:18:41,200 --> 00:18:44,740
Then we picked a day with the smallest current to tow it there.

230
00:18:46,020 --> 00:18:49,080
As I said earlier, this caisson has a double wall.

231
00:18:50,020 --> 00:18:53,620
That became the buoyancy tank, which made it possible to float.

232
00:18:53,621 --> 00:18:59,520
The enormous Kaysan was ready to head out to the choppy seas of the Akashi Strait.

233
00:18:59,980 --> 00:19:03,460
It would be the largest structure of its kind ever towed through these waters

234
00:19:03,461 --> 00:19:07,840
and would face the dual threats of fierce winds and driving seas.

235
00:19:09,880 --> 00:19:14,500
The Akashi Kaikyo Bridge is almost four times as long as the Brooklyn Bridge

236
00:19:14,501 --> 00:19:18,000
or eight times as long as the Sears Tower is high.

237
00:19:18,001 --> 00:19:22,060
The world's longest bridge will return on Modern Marvels.

238
00:19:23,620 --> 00:19:29,840
Central Japan, the turbulent Akashi Strait.

239
00:19:29,841 --> 00:19:36,620
On a clear March day in 1989, 12 powerful tugboats began the perilous job of towing the giant Kaysan,

240
00:19:37,300 --> 00:19:39,400
the first of two to the job site.

241
00:19:39,401 --> 00:19:52,520
Once it was centered over the prepared seabed,

242
00:19:53,120 --> 00:19:55,240
special cables were connected between the caisson

243
00:19:55,241 --> 00:19:57,600
and numerous huge anchors on the seafloor.

244
00:19:59,480 --> 00:20:01,340
These anchors had been dropped in a circle

245
00:20:01,341 --> 00:20:03,200
around the prepared pit in the seabed

246
00:20:03,201 --> 00:20:04,880
and would help to guide the caisson

247
00:20:04,881 --> 00:20:06,720
into the proper position as it sank.

248
00:20:09,400 --> 00:20:13,660
We pulled out a cable from the winch on the top of the sinker, or anchor,

249
00:20:14,160 --> 00:20:18,880
which was already on the seabed, almost 1,000 tons each, and connected it.

250
00:20:19,340 --> 00:20:26,760
This was quite an achievement in itself because you have effectively a cork bobbing in the water

251
00:20:26,761 --> 00:20:29,540
and you're trying to hit a perfect spot.

252
00:20:29,541 --> 00:20:35,360
And through the use of GPS and positioning systems and ballast and weight,

253
00:20:35,361 --> 00:20:41,460
they slowly could sink that caisson down to the prepared seabed.

254
00:20:42,760 --> 00:20:46,500
We started the submerging process the next day at low time.

255
00:20:49,060 --> 00:20:55,880
The submerging work was done by pumping seawater into the core part and lowering it straight down.

256
00:20:58,880 --> 00:21:04,840
There was a monitor on the caisson, and we measured the positioning of the caisson and maneuvered the cables.

257
00:21:05,360 --> 00:21:17,900
Then, about one meter from the seabed, we sank it, with about a two centimeter error.

258
00:21:21,900 --> 00:21:24,740
The huge caisson now rested on the seafloor.

259
00:21:25,240 --> 00:21:27,820
Soon the other one, at the south end of the channel, would follow.

260
00:21:30,080 --> 00:21:34,280
Now, final preparations had to be performed on the silty muck that they covered.

261
00:21:35,360 --> 00:21:43,840
There were some more residues that the grab excavation could not pick up.

262
00:21:43,841 --> 00:21:50,720
After submerging the caisson, we used an underwater robot called Air Lift to suck up some rocks

263
00:21:50,721 --> 00:21:51,720
and rubble.

264
00:21:51,721 --> 00:21:59,580
But once firmly on the bottom, the huge can had to be filled with something other than

265
00:21:59,581 --> 00:22:01,580
seawater to make it heavy.

266
00:22:01,581 --> 00:22:08,580
Once that caisson was in place, all of the water was pumped out, and it was replaced

267
00:22:08,581 --> 00:22:11,520
by special underwater concrete.

268
00:22:11,521 --> 00:22:15,340
This was a new way of doing business for bridge builders.

269
00:22:15,341 --> 00:22:22,240
Traditionally, caissons had been sunk, pumped out, and then men sent inside to work.

270
00:22:22,241 --> 00:22:26,360
Danger was a constant companion, and many perished.

271
00:22:26,361 --> 00:22:31,040
But this caisson had been sunk onto a prepared seafloor, and the only thing entering it would

272
00:22:31,041 --> 00:22:32,473
be this special concrete.

273
00:22:32,474 --> 00:22:36,200
But because the entire structure would be submerged, the Japanese

274
00:22:36,201 --> 00:22:41,981
needed a concrete that could settle and cure underwater.

275
00:22:41,982 --> 00:22:45,800
Enormous concrete plants were floated

276
00:22:45,801 --> 00:22:47,988
to the caisson on barges.

277
00:22:47,989 --> 00:22:53,940
The ingredients were mixed and then pumped in to displace the water.

278
00:22:55,020 --> 00:22:59,039
Each layer poured, used up most of the raw materials on the barge.

279
00:22:59,040 --> 00:23:00,500
As this mixture hardened

280
00:23:00,501 --> 00:23:05,272
underwater, the construction crews would wait for the next favorable tide.

281
00:23:05,273 --> 00:23:06,820
The average wait was two

282
00:23:06,821 --> 00:23:07,722
weeks.

283
00:23:07,723 --> 00:23:21,400
Ultimately, that whole 80 meter diameter caisson, which is about 70 meters high, was filled

284
00:23:21,401 --> 00:23:23,456
completely with concrete.

285
00:23:23,457 --> 00:23:29,460
And then a special table or top was placed on that caisson, about 10 more

286
00:23:29,461 --> 00:23:39,420
meters of concrete, into which was anchored the tower bases themselves.

287
00:23:39,421 --> 00:23:47,520
Once the steel tower bases were cast into that tabletop, then the erection of the towers

288
00:23:47,521 --> 00:23:49,520
began.

289
00:23:49,521 --> 00:23:56,000
The height of the GIST tower is about 300 meters, 1,000 feet.

290
00:23:56,001 --> 00:24:01,840
One of the most important issues for the design of this tower is wind-resistant design.

291
00:24:01,841 --> 00:24:13,660
Because the tower is very high, so it is relatively flexible and easy to vibrate due to the wind.

292
00:24:15,680 --> 00:24:17,680
Many tower designs were considered.

293
00:24:18,300 --> 00:24:24,840
The design chosen was a so-called cruciform, where X-braces would support the openings between the vertical supports.

294
00:24:24,841 --> 00:24:28,820
The purpose of that is really for aerodynamic stability.

295
00:24:29,820 --> 00:24:35,440
It was the most forgiving shape to accommodate the strong forces of the very strong winds that occur.

296
00:24:36,100 --> 00:24:39,940
The tower design also had to incorporate one more major design component.

297
00:24:40,800 --> 00:24:45,720
The two towers had to be able to withstand Japan's frequent and often mighty earthquakes.

298
00:24:46,520 --> 00:24:52,920
From a seismic point of view, it's primarily the towers that's the next or the most critical element

299
00:24:52,921 --> 00:24:54,820
because the towers support the cables.

300
00:24:55,320 --> 00:24:58,900
If there are failures, we could see the towers getting overloaded,

301
00:24:58,901 --> 00:25:02,640
the local buckling of the steel towers at the base.

302
00:25:03,040 --> 00:25:07,060
These are the kind of critical conditions we are designing these structures for.

303
00:25:07,480 --> 00:25:10,940
Each of the 930-foot-high towers was prefabricated

304
00:25:10,941 --> 00:25:13,120
at one of 12 factories across Japan.

305
00:25:13,740 --> 00:25:15,360
They had to match each other perfectly.

306
00:25:16,020 --> 00:25:17,300
Cooperation was critical.

307
00:25:18,440 --> 00:25:22,200
Tolerances needed to be very precise to assure a truly vertical structure.

308
00:25:22,920 --> 00:25:27,291
Tower one shaft consists of 30 blocks vertically.

309
00:25:27,292 --> 00:25:30,680
Both sides of the blocks are polished.

310
00:25:33,200 --> 00:25:38,340
Because one of the most important things for the tower is vertical accuracy.

311
00:25:40,480 --> 00:25:44,740
Each tower component was hauled to the work site by a huge barge-mounted crane.

312
00:25:44,741 --> 00:25:58,660
We use a large tower crane with a capacity of 160 tons, and with that crane we put each block by block.

313
00:26:00,980 --> 00:26:05,480
The slightest deviation would be magnified as the towers reached skyward.

314
00:26:05,481 --> 00:26:18,680
The tower must be perfectly vertical, so you do not induce extraneous forces into the tower as the cables pull one way or another on the towers themselves.

315
00:26:23,260 --> 00:26:30,480
What that translates to is about an inch out of plumb over 900 feet high.

316
00:26:30,481 --> 00:26:40,120
So in order to achieve that accuracy, tremendous tolerances had to be met.

317
00:26:40,640 --> 00:26:45,500
They were perfectly matched so that when you put one on top of the other,

318
00:26:45,940 --> 00:26:48,280
that mating surface is absolutely perfect.

319
00:26:49,400 --> 00:26:51,260
As the construction progressed,

320
00:26:51,780 --> 00:26:57,840
the engineers were required to check the fit on each side of each block of the tower.

321
00:26:58,620 --> 00:27:00,820
Feeler gauges, little shims were used,

322
00:27:01,240 --> 00:27:07,240
and the acceptance was that you could not put that shim or feeler gauge

323
00:27:07,241 --> 00:27:12,020
between the mating surfaces of one block when stacked on top of another.

324
00:27:12,800 --> 00:27:15,200
In essence, the clearance between the tower blocks

325
00:27:15,201 --> 00:27:17,880
had to be thinner than a playing card to pass inspection.

326
00:27:18,700 --> 00:27:23,400
If not, the block would have to be returned to the factory to be further refined.

327
00:27:23,780 --> 00:27:27,140
But none were. The workmanship was that precise.

328
00:27:27,840 --> 00:27:30,520
The weather, however, was not so accommodating.

329
00:27:35,560 --> 00:27:37,080
That was a thunderstorm.

330
00:27:37,480 --> 00:27:42,760
In December of 1992, the northern tower was almost completed,

331
00:27:43,360 --> 00:27:48,100
and this southern tower is about 200 meters high or something.

332
00:27:50,820 --> 00:27:53,880
And that time there was a very large thunderstorm.

333
00:27:53,881 --> 00:28:01,640
it broke the measuring equipment on the cranes on the northern tower and we have to stop the

334
00:28:01,641 --> 00:28:07,880
construction work about three weeks in fact between heavy rain typhoons and rough seas

335
00:28:10,600 --> 00:28:17,040
construction was able to progress only about six days in ten but this had been factored into the

336
00:28:17,041 --> 00:28:23,820
schedule so the overall delay was minimal soon the towers soared gracefully into the skies above

337
00:28:23,821 --> 00:28:25,720
of the Akashi Strait.

338
00:28:25,721 --> 00:28:28,640
But there was one more important device used

339
00:28:28,641 --> 00:28:30,820
to ensure they stayed there.

340
00:28:30,821 --> 00:28:35,780
We decided to put the tuned mass dampers inside the tower.

341
00:28:35,781 --> 00:28:41,060
This is one of the most important things for this tower.

342
00:28:41,061 --> 00:28:43,700
Tuned mass dampers are huge weights

343
00:28:43,701 --> 00:28:45,560
that counteract the forces of wind

344
00:28:45,561 --> 00:28:46,780
or high loads on the bridge.

345
00:28:51,620 --> 00:28:53,600
With constant input from computers,

346
00:28:53,601 --> 00:28:56,040
which are in turn wired to sensors throughout the bridge.

347
00:28:56,720 --> 00:29:00,900
The tuned mass dampers can be set to rock in a direction and at a frequency

348
00:29:00,901 --> 00:29:04,100
that will offset the sway inherent in such tall towers.

349
00:29:04,840 --> 00:29:09,320
Twenty were installed in each tower and worked 24-7 to keep the structures upright.

350
00:29:10,140 --> 00:29:14,740
With the giant towers complete, the next gargantuan task loomed before them,

351
00:29:15,520 --> 00:29:18,300
stringing the three-foot diameter steel cables.

352
00:29:18,301 --> 00:29:30,160
At 350,000 tons, the north anchorage of the Akashikaikyo Bridge weighs nearly as much as four of America's largest aircraft carriers.

353
00:29:30,720 --> 00:29:33,580
The world's longest bridge will return on Modern Marvels.

354
00:29:37,280 --> 00:29:40,660
The spine of a suspension bridge is its cable.

355
00:29:41,120 --> 00:29:43,940
No other component is so graceful or so apparent.

356
00:29:43,941 --> 00:29:49,860
And none other is so crucial to the continuing success of a long-span bridge.

357
00:29:50,440 --> 00:29:56,600
Making the right choices for cabling was one of the greatest challenges facing the designers of the Akashi Kaikyo Bridge.

358
00:29:56,980 --> 00:30:01,340
The difficulty is that if they used traditional materials,

359
00:30:02,060 --> 00:30:08,420
instead of having one main 1.1 meter diameter cable on each side of the bridge,

360
00:30:08,760 --> 00:30:13,580
they would have to have two cables because the material was not strong enough.

361
00:30:13,940 --> 00:30:20,020
What the Japanese did was to develop a higher strength wire strand.

362
00:30:20,460 --> 00:30:26,480
It was about 12% higher than had been used in traditional cable construction for suspension bridges.

363
00:30:27,260 --> 00:30:32,400
In order to increase cable strength, we reviewed the components of the cables

364
00:30:32,401 --> 00:30:38,420
and were able to enhance the cable strength by increasing the amount of silicone in it.

365
00:30:39,000 --> 00:30:41,380
The structure of the cables is seen here.

366
00:30:41,381 --> 00:30:46,760
It is a bundle of 127 wires of 5 millimeters in diameter.

367
00:30:47,440 --> 00:30:48,780
This is called a strand.

368
00:30:49,540 --> 00:30:54,080
290 of these strands make up the cables for the Akashi Kaikyo Bridge.

369
00:30:54,680 --> 00:30:56,140
36,000 wires.

370
00:30:56,900 --> 00:31:00,900
These strands were then rolled up on tremendously large spools

371
00:31:00,901 --> 00:31:03,580
and then stored at the fabrication site.

372
00:31:04,260 --> 00:31:08,440
Now the problem becomes, how do you erect the suspension cable?

373
00:31:11,380 --> 00:31:18,260
The Japanese did not want to interrupt the flow of commerce, 1,400 vehicles a day through the Akashi Straits.

374
00:31:18,560 --> 00:31:27,400
They wanted a means of erecting the cable such that there would be absolutely no inconvenience to the commercial user of the Akashi Straits.

375
00:31:27,860 --> 00:31:35,180
What they did was they decided to go to something called a prefabricated wire system.

376
00:31:35,540 --> 00:31:38,480
With the immense reels of cables sitting at the water's edge,

377
00:31:38,760 --> 00:31:41,780
a unique system was needed to sling the cables across the bridge.

378
00:31:42,000 --> 00:31:46,060
They chose a non-invasive procedure to pull the first cable strand across the strait.

379
00:31:46,061 --> 00:31:50,360
What the Japanese did was they went to these new high-tech materials,

380
00:31:50,580 --> 00:31:53,880
to a fiber composite material that's called an aramid,

381
00:31:54,160 --> 00:31:56,640
about a quarter of an inch diameter cable,

382
00:31:56,880 --> 00:31:59,600
very, very lightweight, very, very strong.

383
00:32:05,180 --> 00:32:14,120
The helicopter actually picked one end of the cable up, took it from one anchorage to

384
00:32:14,121 --> 00:32:28,121
the top of the tower, first tower, top of the second tower, and to the second anchorage

385
00:32:29,240 --> 00:32:32,100
on the Awadji Island.

386
00:32:32,101 --> 00:32:39,480
Other cabling was then added, pulled across the bridge, and that was repeated 290 times

387
00:32:39,481 --> 00:32:48,160
because there were 290 of these prefabricated wire strands in each cable.

388
00:32:48,161 --> 00:32:54,180
After 290 strands were erected, we shaped them into a circular form the way it looks

389
00:32:54,181 --> 00:32:55,380
now.

390
00:32:55,381 --> 00:32:59,260
This process is called squeezing.

391
00:32:59,261 --> 00:33:03,300
the mass of wires was forced into a large circular shape by the cable clamps.

392
00:33:03,760 --> 00:33:08,520
Once this had been done, the suspension hanger ropes could be dropped toward the roadbed area.

393
00:33:09,260 --> 00:33:11,680
But first, the main cable had to be protected.

394
00:33:12,520 --> 00:33:14,500
This, too, would pioneer new technologies.

395
00:33:15,180 --> 00:33:18,000
The huge bundle would be wrapped in yet more steel wire

396
00:33:18,001 --> 00:33:21,960
by a special machine that worked tirelessly, like a metal spider.

397
00:33:22,420 --> 00:33:24,320
But with the covering over the wire,

398
00:33:24,540 --> 00:33:28,600
it was now subjected to the principal enemy of all suspension bridges, corrosion.

399
00:33:29,120 --> 00:33:33,140
That's the big problem, that you don't really know what's going on in the interior.

400
00:33:33,560 --> 00:33:37,300
If we have enough wires then corroding and breaking,

401
00:33:37,480 --> 00:33:40,480
then it could potentially lead to a collapse of the bridge.

402
00:33:40,481 --> 00:33:45,700
To combat corrosion, the Akashi Kaikyo Bridge uses two unique lines of defense.

403
00:33:46,460 --> 00:33:50,980
This was the first time we wrapped rubber around the suspension cable.

404
00:33:51,440 --> 00:33:57,140
And since rubber is intolerant of ultraviolet rays, we painted the rubber to protect it.

405
00:33:57,141 --> 00:34:01,480
The paint was a special blend and a kind that would adhere to rubber.

406
00:34:01,481 --> 00:34:05,620
This sealed the cable bundle against light, wind, moisture, and dirt.

407
00:34:06,020 --> 00:34:11,340
But inside the jacketed cable, almost 20% of the total area was still just empty air.

408
00:34:11,800 --> 00:34:14,060
How could they keep the inside of the cable dry?

409
00:34:14,480 --> 00:34:23,340
Typically what's done on suspension bridges is a very heavy paste is literally spread over the wires,

410
00:34:23,341 --> 00:34:28,100
injected in the wires, and it would tend to fill that 20% of the voids.

411
00:34:30,060 --> 00:34:33,460
The Japanese wanted to control the humidity

412
00:34:33,461 --> 00:34:38,860
because in the absence of humidity, you can control corrosion.

413
00:34:39,500 --> 00:34:46,440
They decided that they would design a dehumidification system for those cables.

414
00:34:46,940 --> 00:34:51,740
They would pump air, dry air, throughout the whole main cable,

415
00:34:51,741 --> 00:34:56,000
which extends more than four kilometers from Anchorage to Anchorage.

416
00:34:56,700 --> 00:35:01,860
This air must be pumped throughout the main cables 24 hours a day, every day of the year.

417
00:35:02,900 --> 00:35:08,080
In this, even more so than with other aspects of the bridge, maintenance is critical.

418
00:35:08,980 --> 00:35:11,840
Inspection of the cable is not easy.

419
00:35:12,360 --> 00:35:18,180
We check the cable by using some kind of thermometer and humidity meter.

420
00:35:18,181 --> 00:35:21,200
We're always checking inside of the cable.

421
00:35:21,740 --> 00:35:28,380
keep the relative humidity under 40 percent.

422
00:35:29,060 --> 00:35:31,480
And this is really kind of unique and ingenious

423
00:35:31,481 --> 00:35:35,480
because if corrosion of those cables can be controlled,

424
00:35:36,040 --> 00:35:38,460
that will be tremendous savings to the public

425
00:35:38,461 --> 00:35:43,720
75 years, 100 years, 150 years down the line

426
00:35:43,721 --> 00:35:48,360
when these cables typically start seeing corrosion-related problems.

427
00:35:48,840 --> 00:35:51,380
The cables were strung the length of the bridge.

428
00:35:51,740 --> 00:35:53,400
and then tied into the anchorages.

429
00:35:54,180 --> 00:35:55,680
Deep inside the anchorage tower

430
00:35:55,681 --> 00:35:58,240
is a receiver frame for the cable strands,

431
00:35:58,580 --> 00:36:00,140
which are affixed one at a time.

432
00:36:01,220 --> 00:36:04,980
We did not fasten all 36,000 lines in one spot.

433
00:36:05,540 --> 00:36:07,880
We placed a socket at the end of the strand,

434
00:36:08,320 --> 00:36:11,060
so each socket is fixed separately on the anchorage.

435
00:36:11,740 --> 00:36:16,220
This was repeated 290 times for each cable on each side.

436
00:36:17,000 --> 00:36:18,080
As one might expect,

437
00:36:18,620 --> 00:36:21,480
security is higher here than at any other site on the branch.

438
00:36:21,740 --> 00:36:28,440
With the towers erected and the cables slung from one anchorage to the other, all that remained was to build the roadbed.

439
00:36:29,180 --> 00:36:37,600
But nature had one final deadly challenge to the completion of the Akashikaikyo Bridge, one that could bring about total destruction.

440
00:36:38,680 --> 00:36:48,220
The Akashikaikyo Bridge uses over 186,000 miles of wire in its suspension cables, enough to encircle the Earth seven and a half times.

441
00:36:48,221 --> 00:36:53,220
The world's longest bridge will return on Modern Marvels.

442
00:36:57,540 --> 00:37:00,140
January 17, 1995.

443
00:37:04,160 --> 00:37:08,180
Kobe, Japan is shattered by a magnitude 7.5 earthquake.

444
00:37:09,360 --> 00:37:12,300
By evening, 5,000 people are dead or missing.

445
00:37:13,040 --> 00:37:16,040
26,000 are injured and the city is in flames.

446
00:37:16,041 --> 00:37:19,920
One-sixth of the population is homeless.

447
00:37:20,680 --> 00:37:25,320
And in the channel, straddling the city-killing fault is the Akashikaikyo Bridge.

448
00:37:25,600 --> 00:37:29,360
The actual fault went right underneath the bridge.

449
00:37:29,820 --> 00:37:32,860
The tower on the Kobe side stood firm.

450
00:37:33,740 --> 00:37:42,860
The tower on the Awaji Island side actually moved some 0.8 meters towards Awaji Island.

451
00:37:42,861 --> 00:37:48,080
and the anchorage on Awaji Island itself moved 1.2 meters.

452
00:37:48,620 --> 00:37:52,780
The type of fault that we're dealing with is a strike-slip fault

453
00:37:52,781 --> 00:37:55,520
where it's a vertical gash into the earth

454
00:37:55,521 --> 00:37:59,280
where one side is moving horizontally past the other side.

455
00:37:59,720 --> 00:38:03,000
In this particular case, there seems to have been actually

456
00:38:03,001 --> 00:38:06,080
two individual strands of the fault,

457
00:38:06,560 --> 00:38:11,300
one that ran into the interior of Japan through Kobe

458
00:38:11,301 --> 00:38:15,820
and then another strand that ran out into Awaji Island.

459
00:38:16,020 --> 00:38:21,260
And those two strands more or less come close to each other right beneath the bridge.

460
00:38:21,660 --> 00:38:27,760
The epicenter of the earthquake where the rupture actually started seems to have been right beneath the bridge.

461
00:38:28,380 --> 00:38:33,320
After the major rescue efforts were complete, attention turned to the Akashikaikyo Bridge.

462
00:38:33,920 --> 00:38:39,260
All the tests had been based on a magnitude 8.5 quake, some 100-plus miles inland,

463
00:38:39,261 --> 00:38:43,100
but it had never been tested for a quake originating directly beneath it.

464
00:38:43,620 --> 00:38:45,900
Had the caisson sunk into the gravelly seabed?

465
00:38:46,440 --> 00:38:50,400
Had the towers, now almost four feet displaced, been damaged?

466
00:38:50,820 --> 00:38:52,520
Could they complete the bridge at all?

467
00:38:57,740 --> 00:39:01,880
A thorough inspection showed the bridge structure and cable to be sound.

468
00:39:03,120 --> 00:39:06,780
Amid the chaos and destruction of Kobe, this was welcome news.

469
00:39:06,781 --> 00:39:15,180
construction continued anew a month after the earthquake in February of 1995 it was time to

470
00:39:15,181 --> 00:39:21,060
install the roadway this too would be accomplished in sections like the tower modules each section

471
00:39:21,061 --> 00:39:25,980
was prefabricated on land and staged near the bridge the trust for the body of the

472
00:39:25,981 --> 00:39:33,960
bridge had been carefully designed and tested for many years via both computer and wind tunnel

473
00:39:33,961 --> 00:39:40,880
Aerodynamics were a major concern because the truss work, more than any other element

474
00:39:40,881 --> 00:39:43,740
in the bridge, would be susceptible to the effect of winds.

475
00:39:44,740 --> 00:39:50,780
A lot of the wind tunnel tests have been carried out to measure the aerodynamic forces for

476
00:39:50,781 --> 00:39:53,120
a variation of the flutter insubilities.

477
00:39:53,620 --> 00:39:58,300
In fact, the government built some of the largest wind tunnels in the world, north of

478
00:39:58,301 --> 00:40:03,200
Tokyo, specifically to test the effects of high winds on the Akashikaikyo Bridge.

479
00:40:03,960 --> 00:40:06,740
A 1-100th scale model was used.

480
00:40:07,160 --> 00:40:13,740
Keep in mind this bridge is 4 kilometers long, so you're looking at about a 40-meter model long.

481
00:40:14,020 --> 00:40:19,120
And they actually put this into a turbulent wind generator,

482
00:40:19,380 --> 00:40:26,140
and they tested that bridge in scaled wind flow up to, I believe it was 80 meters a second.

483
00:40:26,400 --> 00:40:33,340
They learned that the Akashikaikyo Bridge could bow sideways almost 100 feet before experiencing significant damage.

484
00:40:33,960 --> 00:40:40,180
And to do that, the wind would have to be blowing at nearly 200 miles per hour, an unlikely scenario.

485
00:40:41,580 --> 00:40:47,020
The design selected for the roadbed, decking, and truss was a cantilevered truss girder.

486
00:40:48,040 --> 00:40:52,120
Construction of this last major phase of the bridge began in mid-1995.

487
00:40:52,820 --> 00:40:58,460
The roadbed and the girders are built from the towers out,

488
00:40:58,461 --> 00:41:04,780
so that small sections of truss would be brought out, would be put into place,

489
00:41:04,900 --> 00:41:11,980
and then the suspender ropes would be attached to that particular panel of truss.

490
00:41:12,520 --> 00:41:17,460
Once that panel of truss was then suspended by the suspender cable,

491
00:41:18,060 --> 00:41:23,280
they would move on to the next section and bring another truss element out

492
00:41:23,281 --> 00:41:29,240
and cantilever off of the previously erected truss segment.

493
00:41:29,620 --> 00:41:34,180
The open girder design was selected to afford great strength without impeding wind flow.

494
00:41:35,680 --> 00:41:37,340
The roadbed came next.

495
00:41:38,200 --> 00:41:41,560
As the bridge neared completion, the now familiar shape took form.

496
00:41:42,260 --> 00:41:46,420
On top of the truss system, then, you have to place a deck.

497
00:41:46,720 --> 00:41:48,960
And basically what that is is a steel plate.

498
00:41:48,961 --> 00:41:56,240
You come back in and you put a couple of inches of a special kind of asphalt right on top of the steel plate,

499
00:41:56,620 --> 00:42:03,880
and you put another couple of inches of a certain type of epoxy asphalt on top of that.

500
00:42:06,200 --> 00:42:10,660
On April 5, 1998, the massive project was complete.

501
00:42:13,540 --> 00:42:17,180
Ten years and nearly $4 billion had gone into the effort.

502
00:42:17,820 --> 00:42:22,620
There had been six injuries, but because of extensive safety precautions, no fatalities.

503
00:42:23,180 --> 00:42:29,960
This impressive safety record stands as a testament to the efforts of the many companies whose fortunes rode on the Akashikaikyo Bridge.

504
00:42:32,600 --> 00:42:35,800
By mid-morning, the bridge opened for traffic.

505
00:42:36,480 --> 00:42:41,880
1,500 invited guests were joined by tens of thousands of people from all walks of Japanese society.

506
00:42:42,640 --> 00:42:46,840
I had the opportunity to be at that opening ceremony with the crown prince and princess.

507
00:42:47,180 --> 00:42:59,427
And it was an exhilarating experience in just looking at the culmination of probably 30 years worth of work that had gone into the planning, the experimentation, the

508
00:42:59,428 --> 00:43:03,880
design, and finally the construction over the last 10 years.

509
00:43:04,300 --> 00:43:08,700
The bridge creates a graceful sweeping arc across the Akashi Strait today.

510
00:43:09,960 --> 00:43:14,780
But the Akashi-Kaikyo Bridge may be one of the last huge metal suspension bridges made.

511
00:43:14,781 --> 00:43:19,880
As the primary ingredient, steel, reaches the limits of its strength.

512
00:43:20,820 --> 00:43:28,320
That bridge is designed so that 90% of the weight of the bridge supports itself,

513
00:43:28,700 --> 00:43:34,980
which means that only 10% of the total weight of the bridge is dedicated to supporting the traffic.

514
00:43:35,720 --> 00:43:43,780
Previously, suspension bridges have been designed to accommodate about 25%.

515
00:43:44,780 --> 00:43:52,220
A much longer bridge made of steel would be completely ineffective and could eventually collapse under its own weight.

516
00:43:53,220 --> 00:44:04,020
Clearly we need to look for stronger and lighter materials since we are chasing primarily to save the self-weight of the bridge to achieve longer spans.

517
00:44:04,021 --> 00:44:15,900
But for now, the Akashi Kaikyo, part of a vanishing breed of heavy steel bridges, is a lone sentinel holding the world's record for the longest single span.

518
00:44:16,820 --> 00:44:20,640
It stands astride the gateway to Asia like a portal to the future.

519
00:44:21,520 --> 00:44:24,960
It is the pride of Japan, the Akashi Kaikyo Bridge.

